  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Gusty winds today (Saturday), creating choppy conditions for\n   area lakes, especially across northwest Montana.\n\n-  Mild temperatures next week, with shower chances for most areas\n   by Thursday and Friday.\n\n-  Confidence is growing for cooler temperatures by next weekend.\n\nA cold front and upper-level weather system are moving across the Northern\nRockies this afternoon. Other than a few light showers along the Canadian\nborder, this front is mostly producing gusty west to southwest winds across\nwestern Montana and north-central Idaho. The strongest winds are still\nexpected to be across northwest Montana this afternoon and evening, where\ngusts between 25 and 35 mph will be quite common. Anyone planning to be on\narea lakes, especially Flathead Lake, should be aware of choppy conditions\nand persistent winds. A Lake Wind Advisory will remain in place from 2:00\nPM to 7:00 PM.\n\nWeather models remain in strong agreement for a split weather pattern\nSunday into early next week. This pattern will be characterized by a low\npressure system diving south along the west coast and settling along the\ncentral California coast, while a broad low pressure system and the jet\nstream remain positioned in Alaska and northwest Canada. This pattern will\nprovide mild fall temperatures, with warm afternoons and colder morning\ntemperatures, along with light winds. Isolated showers return to the\nforecast across Lemhi County, ID and southwest Montana by Tuesday and\nWednesday as moisture begins to stream northward from the low near\nCalifornia.\n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase Thursday into Friday. Weather\nmodels suggest the Canadian system will begin to dip southward and\npotentially merge with the low over California as it begins to lift\nnorthward. The exact timing of these systems merging remains the largest\nquestion mark. Around half of the weather models bring widespread shower\nactivity across the Northern Rockies by Thursday, while the remaining\nmodels suggest activity focused further south. By Friday into Saturday,\naround 90% of the models suggest a low pressure system will pass through,\nwith chances of precipitation increasing along with colder\ntemperatures.",
